{"authored" [{"question" "q-79811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0.5,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0.5,1) & =2\\cdot 0.5\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0.5+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-0.5=-0.5, \\\\ Δy & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-0.5)+1\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c388c1ed8737218b3f157b217ff4731b172ace8aed19307b31a77aa2ed9997c6" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Estimate $(1.06)^{4.1}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{y}$ at the point $(1,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068753/state.json" "file_sha256" "490631e9caae6aff35de462ff8f0e18814519090b9886a5251afc49e65b0424e" "basis" 2353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068753/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =4\\cdot {1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{1}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈4\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n(1.06)^{4.1}=f(1.06,4.1),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1.06,4.1).$\n\nMoving from $(1,4)$ to $(1.06,4.1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.06-1=0.06, \\\\ Δy & =4.1-4=0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈4\\cdot (0.06)+0\\cdot (0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.24+0 \\\\  & ≈0.24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.24.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.06,4.1) & ≈f(1,4)+0.24 \\\\ (1.06)^{4.1} & ≈{1}^{4}+0.24 \\\\  & ≈1+0.24 \\\\  & ≈1.24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068753/state.json" "file_sha256" "490631e9caae6aff35de462ff8f0e18814519090b9886a5251afc49e65b0424e" "basis" 2353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068753/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/140687